  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Four floors of plague, cholera and Spanish flu, shown in the second summer of Covid.</p>
    <p>
      “Stayin’ Alive – mit Seuchen leben” filled the Turm zur Katz in Konstanz with the history of epidemics and what
      they did to societies. It was framed as a fictional research institute with four departments, one per floor: data
      collection, research, management and a real-world lab. Historians, computer scientists, architects, music and
      communication designers built it together, about 80 students in all.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 2021 a visitor could not be asked to touch shared screens or crowd around a guide. Most of the content had to
      come to each visitor on their own device.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">One iPad that carried the whole exhibition, apart from the texts on the walls.</p>
    <p>
      Every visitor got an iPad at the entrance. The UX team for the whole exhibition was two people, and I was one of
      its two leads. We designed the guide, led the computer science students who built it, and I worked on the concept
      of the ground floor, where the institute collects data about its visitors, and on the augmented reality that turns
      objects into stations. The historians wrote the content; our part was to make it usable by anyone who walks in.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="stayin-floors" ratio="1200 / 560" narrow="600 / 1000" />
      <figcaption>
        One iPad for all four floors: it measured the visitor on the ground floor, showed five diseases in AR, ran the
        simulation and asked for an answer in their own words (schematic section, times illustrative).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image
        src={room}
        alt="Ground floor, department A: a dark blue room with a pin board, panels on visiting time, tracking and risk contacts, a black counter with places for the iPads and monitors of the database"
        widths={full(widths, room)}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>Ground floor, department A: data collection. The visitor is the first data point.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="c-pair" style={row(arBody, arWall)}>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={arBody}
          alt="A visitor holds an iPad over a glowing body model; the augmented reality shows the course of a disease, with tabs for plague, cholera, Spanish flu, malaria and Covid"
          widths={full(widths, arBody)}
          sizes={half}
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>First floor: the course of five diseases, in augmented reality on a body model.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={arWall}
