        <figcaption>The pin board: visitors' comments live on the wall, but only on the iPad.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">The iPad as the institute's instrument, not as a second screen.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>The visitor becomes part of the data.</strong> On the ground floor the institute measures its own visitors:
      how long they stay on each floor compared with the fastest and the slowest, which risk contacts they had. It made
      the exhibition's subject personal before the history began.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Hands on the table, not on a shared screen.</strong> On the management floor a simulation asks what you
      would decree when an epidemic arrives, work, schools, hospitals, and shows infections and the R value change. It
      runs on the iPad, laid into a table map of the city.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ending with a question, not an answer.</strong> On the top floor cut-out figures ask “Divide?”, “Doubt?”,
      “Isolate?”, “Stand together?”, and the iPad asks the visitor to answer in their own words.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair" style={row(uiStats, uiSim)}>
